solution:
One pass is enough. Remember where the current climb started; every time the trail goes up, measure the gain from that start; every time it doesn't, the next climb starts here.

```js
function biggestClimb(heights) {
  let best = 0;
  let start = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < heights.length; i++) {
    if (heights[i] > heights[i - 1]) {
      best = Math.max(best, heights[i] - heights[start]);
    } else {
      start = i;
    }
  }
  return best;
}
```

A flat step (`2, 2`) resets the start, because "strictly higher" fails. Measuring gain rather than counting steps matters: in `[0, 100, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]` the one-step climb gains 100, more than the six-step climb's 5. Starting `best` at 0 covers empty arrays, single readings and trails that only go down.
